WEBVTT

00:00:01.200 --> 00:00:08.840
Hi, this is Dr. Jelena Trajkovic, CECS Department CSULB. I would like to show you a couple of

00:00:08.840 --> 00:00:17.020
useful tips and tricks for running the simulation. Here in the window, you can see that I modified

00:00:17.020 --> 00:00:25.760
the testbench for our first lab assignment. And what I did is, first, I extended the time

00:00:25.760 --> 00:00:33.120
of weight between, I changed the value of your each input, and also I added two new test cases.

00:00:33.120 --> 00:00:41.170
So when I run the simulation, it's going to take a little while, what you're going to

00:00:41.170 --> 00:00:53.780
see is that simulator is not going to be able in this first default time to show all the

00:00:53.780 --> 00:01:01.060
test cases. So here only first test case, the second case appears. This is because when

00:01:01.060 --> 00:01:11.120
you run simulation, simulation runs for predefined default time. You can click in your TCL console

00:01:11.120 --> 00:01:20.540
and use the console command run. And let's say we are going to run for another 1000 nanoseconds

00:01:20.540 --> 00:01:27.900
and press enter, and again we are going to do zoom fit, and here we are going to be able

00:01:27.900 --> 00:01:39.290
to see all other test cases. We can repeat this again, so by pressing an arrow up key

00:01:39.290 --> 00:01:45.090
you can repeat the command, and now if you zoom fit you're going to see that nothing

00:01:45.090 --> 00:01:54.450
happened because after this initial, we didn't modify any other value of the inputs. The other

00:01:54.450 --> 00:02:02.530
thing that I wanted to show you is adding the signals. So here in this name pane, if you click

00:02:02.530 --> 00:02:09.490
on your unit under test, you can add those signals to the wave. What you might be interesting

00:02:09.490 --> 00:02:16.370
to find, it's within the module itself that you are testing or that you

00:02:16.370 --> 00:02:21.810
instantiated, what are the values, intermediate values inside. So right now

00:02:21.810 --> 00:02:27.010
we don't see that because we added the signals after we started simulation.

00:02:27.010 --> 00:02:33.490
In order to see the signals inside we can do restart.

00:02:33.490 --> 00:02:39.810
And this is also in TCL console. And when we do restart we can

00:02:39.810 --> 00:02:53.010
again run for a certain amount of time. Let's do for another and zoom fit and you're going to see

00:02:53.010 --> 00:03:03.090
the values of what is inside of your UUT. The second thing that I showed you may not be of

00:03:03.090 --> 00:03:10.050
interest right now to you, but when we start building more complex modules out of the simplest

00:03:10.050 --> 00:03:16.370
modules, we would like to see the connections and we would like to see the insights of more

00:03:16.370 --> 00:03:22.370
complex modules and in that case this would be a very useful thing to do.

